<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constaffel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from <a href="/years/1336">1336</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constaffel was the corporation of the nobility, knights, rentiers and leading merchants of Zürich, created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-brun">Rudolf Brun</a>'s constitution of 1336 alongside the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. It sent members to the city's Small and Large Councils and was the political home of the old patrician families. It survives today as a social society that takes part in the Sechseläuten festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constaffel.jpg" alt="Constaffel" width="320" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CHE_Gesellschaft_zur_Constaffel_COA.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In legal texts the word Konstaffel meant the residents of a castle, city or city quarter, and in Strasbourg it also named an institution of the upper class. In Zürich, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-brun">Rudolf Brun</a> gathered the urban upper class into the Constaffel during his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> revolution of 1336. At first it was a personal association with no internal constitution, made up, as in Strasbourg, of members of the upper-class drinking rooms; Brun chose from it thirteen members of the council, who sat facing an equal number of guild masters. It is comparable to the Achtburger of Basel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1348 the Society of Nobles, part of the Constaffel, built the Haus zum Rüden, which served as the city hall until 1401 and as the Constaffel's assembly house. In 1417 came the Gemeine Konstaffel, a fraternity with military, charitable and religious purposes. From the end of the 14th century the body declined through emigration, social decline and the dying out of leading families, reaching its lowest point in the Old Zurich War.</p><p>After the Waldmann affair, the Fourth Oath Letter of 1489 made it a political <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, and in 1490 the council required it to take into the Gemeine Konstaffel all men and women of the city without guild rights, among them the residents of the Kratzquartier. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the drinking society became the closed Noble Society zum Rüden, dissolved in 1878; from 1600 it gave guild families only conditional membership as Stubenhitzer, who reached the council seats through a new Bürgerliche Konstaffel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Constaffel was dissolved in 1798, restored in 1803 and 1815 as one of thirteen cantonal and municipal electoral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, and dissolved again in 1838 and 1866. In 1841 it formed itself into the Gesellschaft zur Konstaffel, a society whose purpose is to take part in the Sechseläuten.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constaffel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1336 the guilds organised economic life, regulating crafts and prices, while the <b>Constaffel</b> brought together the old patrician families.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
